## The short version

In about **one page**, pick a license for the project you're currently imagining, explain in plain terms what it lets others do (and not do) and why it fits your goals, and name **one governance choice** you'd make as the project's founder — and why.

---

## Why this assignment exists

The moment you publish code, the law has an opinion about it — and the default opinion is *all rights reserved*: without a license, even public code on GitHub can't legally be used or modified by anyone. Founders don't get to treat licensing as fine print, because the license **is** a product decision: it determines who can adopt your work, whether companies will touch it, and whether derivatives stay open. Governance is the same kind of decision one level up — the rules that let strangers contribute safely.

This assignment makes you practice that founder's move once, cheaply, *before* it counts: reasoning from your project's goals to a legal and social structure, in plain language. In Week 8 your team will choose a license and governance norms for real, and this response is the thinking you'll bring to that table. Beyond the course, this is literacy every working engineer needs — you will use, evaluate, and someday release licensed software for the rest of your career, and "I just picked MIT because everyone does" is not a professional answer.

---

## What to produce

A single page (Markdown or PDF) with three parts:

**1. Your license, named.** Pick a real license (MIT, Apache-2.0, GPL-3.0, AGPL-3.0, MPL-2.0, BSD…) for your most promising candidate idea. Use [choosealicense.com](https://choosealicense.com) and the <course-link type="page" id="pg-license-cheat-sheet">License Cheat Sheet</course-link>.

**2. What it means, in plain terms.** Two short paragraphs: what your license lets others **do** (use, modify, sell, embed?), what it **requires** of them (keep notices? share changes? same license?), and **why that fits your goals** — do you want maximum adoption (permissive) or a guarantee the ecosystem stays open (copyleft)? If patents could plausibly matter for your project, say whether that pushed you toward an explicit patent grant (e.g., Apache-2.0).

**3. One governance choice.** Name one call you'd make as founder — code of conduct, contribution process, who reviews and merges, where discussion happens, solo decision vs. small council — and give a sentence or two of *why*, from the perspective of a stranger deciding whether to contribute.

---

## Requirements & constraints

- **Individual work**, about one page. Concision counts.
- **A real license and a real reason.** "MIT because it's popular" scores as no reasoning; "MIT because I want maximum adoption and don't care if forks go closed" is reasoning.
- **Plain terms** — write for a classmate, not a lawyer. (This is general background, not legal advice, and yours doesn't need to be either.)
- **AI use:** fine for checking your understanding of a license's terms; the choice and reasoning must be yours.

---

## How it's graded

Completion-graded against three checks (reach all three for full credit):

| Check | What it looks like |
|---|---|
| **A defensible choice** | A real license, named, matched to a stated goal (adoption vs. openness vs. patent protection) |
| **Accurate plain-language reading** | What it permits and requires is essentially correct — no "GPL means no one can sell it" myths |
| **A reasoned governance call** | One concrete choice with a contributor's-eye rationale |

---

## Tips and common pitfalls

- **Reason from your goal, not from popularity.** The right question is "what do I want to happen to this code?"
- **Copyleft isn't anti-commercial** — companies use and sell GPL software; they just must keep derivatives open. Get the requirement right.
- **The patent column matters more than it looks** if your project could ever attract company users — see the cheat sheet's Gotcha.
- **Governance is for strangers.** Pick the choice that most affects someone who's never met you deciding whether to help.

